  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe strategy combines gradient-based optimization with an evolutionary search to escape local minima and explore the solution space more thoroughly.\nKey idea:\n- Use a population of candidate solutions\n- Mutate them using gradient information\n- Combine global and local search\n- Leverage initial solution as a seed\nTackles:\n- Non-convexity\n- Multiple local minima\nLimitations:\n- Higher computational cost\n- Requires careful parameter tuning\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Variables: h \u2208 [0,1]^n with \u2211h = n/2\n- Objective: min max_{k} \u2211 h_i (1 - h_{i+k}) dx\n- Smoothing: Use logistic function to approximate max\n- Substitute: C5 = max_k [ \u2211 h(x) (1 - h(x+k)) dx ]\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na. Initialization\n   - If initial_h_values exists, use it\n   - Else, create a step function with first half 1's\nb. Fitness evaluation\n   - Compute C5 using np.correlate\nc. Evolutionary loop\n   1. Generate population from best solution\n   2. Compute fitness for all\n   3. Apply mutations using gradients\n   4. Perform selection\n   5. Update best solution\nd. Gradient computation\n   - Smooth max approximation\n   - Finite differences for derivative\ne. Constraint handling\n   - Clip to [0,1], rebalance sum\nf. Termination\n   - Time budget exhaustion\n   - No improvement for k steps\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n- Population size: 20-40\n- Mutation rate: 0.1-0.2\n- Smoothing beta: 10-20\n- Selection pressure: tournament size 2-5\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Time: 600s main loop, 200s gradient descent, 100s polishing\n- Memory: ~50MB for n=70\n- Adapt: reduce population if time short\n\n6. Validation and return\n- Final C5 evaluation\n- Check feasibility\n- Return: h_values, C5, n",
